[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# Solitaire Design Lessons\n\n## Search Space and Control\n- Treat redeals as a major complexity multiplier. Unlimited or easy recycling can turn otherwise reasonable games into cyclic search problems, especially if winning also requires exhausting the stock or waste.\n- When a solver times out by revisiting many states, first remove reversible loops and redundant move paths before loosening the game's core constraints.\n- Restricting moves to pile tops is a strong default for face-up tableau games; allowing extraction from deep inside piles often creates many equivalent branches without adding meaningful decisions.\n- In visible-layout games that time out rather than dead-end, reduce low-value branching before adding more freedom. Reversible tableau moves, unrestricted empty-pile fills, and moves that pull cards back out of goal piles often create \"parking\" states without improving the core puzzle.\n- Tightening empty-pile entry rules is one of the cleanest ways to cut search noise when empties mainly serve as reversible parking. Limit who may enter empties before adding broader mobility.\n\n## Movement Rules and Mobility\n- In fully visible layouts, timeout-heavy losses usually indicate weak local reorganization more than weak card access. Improve internal mobility before adding redeals or other broad access tools.\n- Prefer small, targeted mobility boosts over global freedom. A limited temporary storage cell, a narrow tableau-to-tableau transport rule, or a small recovery move often improves solvability more cleanly than broad new move types.\n- Make transport rules looser than completion rules when the goal is to assemble strict runs, sets, or ordered sequences. If movement demands the same pattern as final completion, the game often stalls before its main puzzle appears.\n- Empty spaces should either be tightly restricted to prevent reversible clutter or deliberately serve as useful transport toward the objective. Avoid leaving them broadly open without a clear strategic role.\n- Holding cells, reserves, or temporary storage should usually connect directly to the scoring path, not only to transport. If stored cards must be reintroduced through extra tableau moves before they can score, the game tends to become search-heavy and inefficient.\n- When a visible game times out, prefer mobility changes that advance objective progress directly\u2014such as allowing temporary storage to feed foundations\u2014rather than broadly loosening tableau movement.\n- Keep scoring moves flexible while keeping temporary tableau transport relatively narrower when possible; this preserves progress toward the goal while reducing reversible branch growth.\n\n## Rules Clarity and Objectives\n- Avoid overlapping move rules for the same source and destination. Express them as one clear condition tree rather than separate special-case transports, since duplicate pathways create both rules ambiguity and implementation conflicts.\n- Check win conditions for harmful interactions with recycling rules. Objectives that require fully exhausting the stock become much harder and more repetitive when players can loop through the same cards indefinitely.\n- Build rules and objective rules should complement each other rather than duplicate each other's strictness. Let the challenge come from planning toward the goal, not from blocking all intermediate reorganization.\n- In fully visible games, allowing cards to move back out of goal or scoring piles should be an exception, not a default. Regress moves often add far more reversible search than strategic depth.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 4 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solver times out in a Klondike-like game with cells, first cut the most reversible parking route rather than adding more access. Temporary storage is much healthier when it connects directly to objective progress than when it also supports broad tableau reshuffling.\n2. When a visible-layout game already wins often but occasional losses hit a search cap, reduce branching on progress moves before adding more freedom. In particular, letting temporary storage feed the goal directly can create many equivalent scoring orders; routing goal moves through a primary area instead can preserve strategy while shrinking the solver\u2019s state space.\n3. When a visible-layout solitaire mostly times out instead of dead-ending, add a small amount of high-value temporary storage before loosening tableau rules broadly. A few cells can unlock buried sequences with much less reversible search than making every tableau pile a freer parking space.\n4. When a deck is effectively single-suit, repeating suit-based completion constraints can add rule text without adding real decisions. In stalled tableau-builders, simplify the scoring rule first if it removes redundancy, before loosening the whole transport system.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# Solitaire Design Lessons\n\n## Search Space and Control\n- Treat redeals as a major complexity multiplier. Unlimited or easy recycling can turn otherwise reasonable games into cyclic search problems, especially if winning also requires exhausting the stock or waste.\n- When a solver times out by revisiting many states, first remove reversible loops and redundant move paths before loosening the game's core constraints.\n- Restricting moves to pile tops is a strong default for face-up tableau games; allowing extraction from deep inside piles often creates many equivalent branches without adding meaningful decisions.\n- In visible-layout games that time out rather than dead-end, reduce low-value branching before adding more freedom. Reversible tableau moves, unrestricted empty-pile fills, broad parking routes through cells, and moves that pull cards back out of goal piles often create many equivalent states without improving the core puzzle.\n- Tightening empty-pile entry rules is one of the cleanest ways to cut search noise when empties mainly serve as reversible parking. Limit who may enter empties before adding broader mobility.\n\n## Movement Rules and Mobility\n- In fully visible layouts, timeout-heavy losses usually indicate weak local reorganization more than weak card access. Improve internal mobility before adding redeals or other broad access tools.\n- Prefer small, targeted mobility boosts over global freedom. A few holding cells, a narrow tableau-to-tableau transport rule, or a small recovery move often improves solvability more cleanly than broad new move types.\n- When a visible game mostly times out instead of dead-ending, add a little high-value temporary storage before broadly loosening tableau rules. Limited cells usually unlock buried cards with less reversible search than making every pile a freer parking space.\n- Make transport rules looser than completion rules when the goal is to assemble strict runs, sets, or ordered sequences. If movement demands the same pattern as final completion, the game often stalls before its main puzzle appears.\n- Empty spaces should either be tightly restricted to prevent reversible clutter or deliberately serve as useful transport toward the objective. Avoid leaving them broadly open without a clear strategic role.\n- Holding cells, reserves, or temporary storage should have a narrow, intentional role. If they support both broad tableau reshuffling and direct scoring, they often become the dominant source of reversible branching.\n- Keep scoring moves and transport routes distinct in purpose. If direct cell-to-goal or reserve-to-goal moves create many equivalent scoring orders, route progress through a primary play area instead and reserve storage for setup rather than bypass.\n- In fully visible games, allowing cards to move back out of goal or scoring piles should be an exception, not a default. Regress moves often add far more reversible search than strategic depth.\n\n## Rules Clarity and Objectives\n- Avoid overlapping move rules for the same source and destination. Express them as one clear condition tree rather than separate special-case transports, since duplicate pathways create both rules ambiguity and implementation conflicts.\n- Check win conditions for harmful interactions with recycling rules. Objectives that require fully exhausting the stock become much harder and more repetitive when players can loop through the same cards indefinitely.\n- Build rules and objective rules should complement each other rather than duplicate each other's strictness. Let the challenge come from planning toward the goal, not from blocking all intermediate reorganization.\n- Remove redundant constraints that do not create real choices. In effectively single-suit or otherwise uniform games, repeating suit-based completion restrictions often adds rule text without adding decision-making.\n- When a stalled game\u2019s scoring rule repeats information already enforced by the deck or by movement structure, simplify the objective before loosening the whole transport system.\n```"}]